<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-variables" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Variables</title>

	<p>
		A <term>variable</term> in MATLAB is a named location in memory that stores a value.
		Variables allow you to save data so that it can be reused, modified, and combined with
		other data as a program runs. Every calculation you perform in the
		<xref ref="sec-command-window" text="custom">Command Window</xref> produces values,
		and variables allow you to store those values for later use.
	</p>

	<p>
		At any moment, MATLAB keeps track of all defined variables in an internal memory area
		called the <term>workspace</term>. You can view these variables in the Workspace panel
		of the <xref ref="sec-matlab-desktop" text="custom">MATLAB Desktop</xref>.
	</p>

	<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-variables">
		<title>Variable Assignment in MATLAB</title>

		<p>Syntax: <pf>variable_name = expression</pf></p>

		<p>
			Key rules:
			<ul>
				<li>The expression on the right-hand side is evaluated first; the result is stored under the name on the left-hand side.</li>
				<li>Variable names must start with a letter, may contain letters, digits, and underscores, and cannot be a MATLAB keyword.</li>
				<li>Names are case-sensitive: <pf>Area</pf> and <pf>area</pf> are different variables.</li>
				<li>Ending a line with a semicolon <pf>;</pf> suppresses output in the Command Window but still creates the variable.</li>
				<li>MATLAB stores the computed value, <em>not</em> the formula; changing one variable does not automatically update others that were computed from it.</li>
			</ul>
		</p>
	</assemblage>

	<!-- ============================================================ -->
	<!--	2. Examples													-->
	<!-- ============================================================ -->

	<example xml:id="example-variables-basic-assignment">
		<title>Basic Variable Assignment</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Define the variables <pf>a</pf> and <pf>b</pf> with values <pf>1</pf> and
				<pf>7.8</pf>, and suppress the output of <pf>b</pf>.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
<code>
a = 1
b = 7.8;
</code>
			</program>

			<p>
				After running these commands:
				<ul>
					<li>Both <pf>a</pf> and <pf>b</pf> appear in the Workspace with their assigned values.</li>
					<li>Only the value of <pf>a</pf> is printed in the Command Window because <pf>b</pf> ends with a semicolon.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</statement>
	</example>

	<example xml:id="example-variables-multiple">
		<title>Using Multiple Variables in a Computation</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				A triangle has a base of <pf>4</pf> and a height of <pf>10</pf>.
				Store each measurement in a variable and compute the area.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
<code>
base	 = 4;
height = 10;
area	 = 0.5 * base * height
</code>
			</program>

			<p>
				MATLAB displays <pf>area = 20</pf>. The variables <pf>base</pf> and
				<pf>height</pf> remain in the workspace unchanged. If you now run
				<pf>base = 6;</pf> the value of <pf>area</pf> stays at <pf>20</pf>
				because MATLAB stored the result, not the formula.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</example>

	<example xml:id="example-variables-naming">
		<title>Valid and Descriptive Variable Names</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				The following code uses descriptive names to compute the total cost of a
				full tank of gas.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
<code>
pricePerGallon = 3.11;
tankCapacity	 = 15;
fullTankCost	 = pricePerGallon * tankCapacity
</code>
			</program>

			<p>
				Each name starts with a letter, uses only letters and digits (mixed case),
				avoids MATLAB keywords, and clearly describes the stored value.
				Compare this to naming the variables <pf>x</pf>, <pf>y</pf>, <pf>z</pf>:
				the descriptive version is far easier to read and debug.
			</p>

			<p>
				The following names are <em>invalid</em> and cause an error:
				<ul>
					<li><pf>3sides</pf> — starts with a digit.</li>
					<li><pf>tank-capacity</pf> — contains a hyphen (interpreted as minus).</li>
					<li><pf>end</pf> — is a reserved MATLAB keyword.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</statement>
	</example>

	<exercise label="vars-examples-practice">
		<statement>
			<p>
				A rectangle has a length of <pf>8</pf> and a width of <pf>3</pf>.
				Write MATLAB commands that store each dimension in a descriptively named
				variable, then compute and display the perimeter
				(<m>P = 2(\ell + w)</m>).
				Suppress the intermediate variables.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
<code>
rectLength = 8;
rectWidth	= 3;
perimeter	= 2 * (rectLength + rectWidth)
</code>
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<!-- ============================================================ -->
	<!--	3. Practice Activities										 -->
	<!-- ============================================================ -->

	<exercise>
		<title>Working With Variables</title>
		
		<task label="vars-practice-1">
			<title>Assigning and Displaying Variables</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					In the code cell below, create three variables:
					<ul>
						<li><pf>x</pf> with value <pf>5</pf></li>
						<li><pf>y</pf> with value <pf>-2.5</pf> (suppressed)</li>
						<li><pf>z</pf> equal to <pf>x + y</pf> (displayed)</li>
					</ul>
					Verify that <pf>z</pf> displays as <pf>2.5</pf>.
				</p>
				<sage language="octave">
					<input>
						%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%
						
						%───────────────────────────%
					</input>
				</sage>
			</statement>
			<solution component="example-solution">
				<program language="matlab">
					<code>
					x = 5
					y = -2.5;
					z = x + y
					</code>
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>

		<task label="vars-practice-2">
			<title>Reassigning a Variable</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Run the following sequence and predict each displayed value before checking:
					<ol>
						<li>Assign <pf>n = 10</pf> (display it).</li>
						<li>Assign <pf>n = n + 5</pf> (display it).</li>
						<li>Assign <pf>n = n * 2</pf> (display it).</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<sage language="octave">
					<input>
						%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%
						
						%───────────────────────────%
					</input>
				</sage>
			</statement>
			<solution component="example-solution">
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
n = 10
n = n + 5
n = n * 2
</code>
				</program>
				<p>Expected output: <pf>10</pf>, then <pf>15</pf>, then <pf>30</pf>.</p>
			</solution>
		</task>

		<task label="vars-practice-3">
			<title>Descriptive Names and Workspace</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A cylindrical water tank has a radius of <pf>1.2</pf> meters and a height of
					<pf>3.5</pf> meters. Using descriptive variable names, compute its volume
					<m>V = \pi r^2 h</m>. Use <pf>pi</pf> for <m>\pi</m>.
				</p>
				<sage language="octave">
					<input>
						%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%
						
						%───────────────────────────%
					</input>
				</sage>
			</statement>
			<solution component="example-solution">
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
radius = 1.2;
height = 3.5;
volume = pi * radius^2 * height
</code>
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>

		<task label="vars-practice-4">
			<title>Swap Two Variables</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Given <pf>a = 3</pf> and <pf>b = 7</pf>, write code that swaps their values
					so that afterwards <pf>a = 7</pf> and <pf>b = 3</pf>.
					You will need a temporary variable.
				</p>
				<sage language="octave">
					<input>
						a = 3;
						b = 7;
						%─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───%
						
						%───────────────────────────%
					</input>
				</sage>
			</statement>
			<solution component="example-solution">
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
a = 3;
b = 7;
temp = a;
a = b;
b = temp;
a
b
</code>
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<!-- ============================================================ -->
	<!--	4. Reading Questions										 -->
	<!-- ============================================================ -->

	<exercise label="vars-struct-ckpts">
		<title>Reading Questions</title>

		<task label="vars-struct-ckpt-1">
			<statement correct="no">
				<p>
					In MATLAB, the symbol <c>=</c> means the same thing as in a math equation.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					In MATLAB, <c>=</c> is the <em>assignment operator</em>. It instructs MATLAB to
					evaluate the right-hand side and store the result under the left-hand name. It
					does not represent a mathematical equality.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</task>

		<task label="vars-struct-ckpt-2">
			<statement>
				<p>
					What happens when you end a MATLAB assignment statement with a semicolon?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>The variable is not created in the workspace.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>The variable is still created; the semicolon only affects display.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>The variable is created, but its value is not printed in the Command Window.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! The semicolon suppresses Command Window output while the variable is still stored.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>The variable is created with an empty value.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>The expression is fully evaluated; the semicolon only suppresses display.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>The assignment applies only to the current session and is not saved.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Variables persist in the workspace until cleared or MATLAB is closed, regardless of semicolons.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<task label="vars-struct-ckpt-3">
			<statement>
				<p>
					After running <pf>area = 0.5 * base * height;</pf>, you reassign <pf>base = 10</pf>.
					What is the new value of <pf>area</pf>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>It stays the same — MATLAB does not recompute <pf>area</pf> automatically.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! MATLAB stored the numeric result, not the formula. Changing <pf>base</pf> has no effect on <pf>area</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>It is updated automatically because <pf>base</pf> changed.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB stores values, not formulas. Reassigning <pf>base</pf> does not trigger a recomputation of <pf>area</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>MATLAB throws an error because <pf>base</pf> was already used.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Variables can be freely reassigned. No error occurs.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>The value of <pf>area</pf> becomes undefined.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p><pf>area</pf> retains the value computed from the original <pf>base</pf> and <pf>height</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<task label="rq-command-window-8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A student types the following:
				</p>
				<program language="matlab">
					result = 8 * 3;
				</program>
				<p>
					What is true about this command?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>The variable <pf>result</pf> is created with value 24, but nothing is displayed in the Command Window.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct. The semicolon suppresses output. The variable is created and stored, but the display is silent.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>The variable <pf>result</pf> is not created because of the semicolon.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>A semicolon does not prevent variable creation. It only suppresses the display.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>The variable <pf>result</pf> is created and <c>result = 24</c> is displayed.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>The semicolon suppresses the display. The variable is created but not shown.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>MATLAB displays an error because of the missing output.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>A semicolon is valid MATLAB syntax. No error is produced.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<!-- Q1 MC -->
		<task label="vars-rq-1">
			<statement>
				<p>Which of the following is a valid MATLAB variable name?</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p><pf>2ndValue</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Variable names must start with a letter, not a digit.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>value2nd</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! It starts with a letter and contains only letters and digits.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p><pf>end</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p><pf>end</pf> is a reserved MATLAB keyword and cannot be used as a variable name.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p><pf>my-value</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Hyphens are not allowed in variable names; MATLAB would interpret <pf>-</pf> as subtraction.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<!-- Q2 MC -->
		<task label="vars-rq-2">
			<statement>
				<p>
					After running the two lines below, what is the value of <pf>result</pf>?
				</p>
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
x = 4;
result = x * 3;
x = 10;
</code>
				</program>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>12</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! <pf>result</pf> was computed when <pf>x = 4</pf>. Changing <pf>x</pf> afterward has no effect.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p><pf>30</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB does not recompute <pf>result</pf> when <pf>x</pf> changes later.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p><pf>10</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p><pf>result</pf> stores the product <pf>x * 3</pf> evaluated at the time of the assignment.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>An error occurs.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>No error; all three lines are valid MATLAB assignments.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<!-- Q3 MC -->
		<task label="vars-rq-3">
			<statement>
				<p>What is the purpose of the MATLAB workspace?</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>It stores all currently defined variables and their values.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! The workspace is MATLAB's in-memory record of all active variables.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>It saves MATLAB scripts to disk automatically.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>The workspace holds variables in memory; saving scripts requires explicit file operations.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>It displays Command Window output history.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>That is the role of the Command Window itself, not the workspace panel.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>It manages MATLAB's toolbox licenses.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>License management is handled elsewhere. The workspace stores variables.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<!-- Q4 MC -->
		<task label="vars-rq-4">
			<statement>
				<p>Which command lists all MATLAB reserved keywords?</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>iskeyword</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! Running <pf>iskeyword</pf> with no argument returns the full list of reserved keywords.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p><pf>keywords</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>This is not a standard MATLAB command. Use <pf>iskeyword</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p><pf>whos</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p><pf>whos</pf> lists workspace variables, not keywords.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p><pf>help reserved</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>There is no <pf>reserved</pf> help topic. Use <pf>iskeyword</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<!-- Q5 MC -->
		<task label="vars-rq-5">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which of the following best describes <em>why</em> descriptive variable names are
					recommended?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>They make code easier to read, understand, and debug.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! Names like <pf>tankRadius</pf> immediately convey meaning; names like <pf>r</pf> do not.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>They make MATLAB execute code faster.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Variable name length has no effect on execution speed.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>They prevent accidental reassignment of variables.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB does not restrict reassignment based on name style.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>They allow MATLAB to auto-generate documentation.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB does not automatically generate documentation from variable names.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<!-- Q6 MC -->
		<task label="vars-rq-6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Are <pf>Temperature</pf> and <pf>temperature</pf> the same variable in MATLAB?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>Yes, MATLAB ignores capitalization in variable names.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB is case-sensitive; capitalization matters.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>No, MATLAB variable names are case-sensitive.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! <pf>Temperature</pf> and <pf>temperature</pf> are two distinct variables.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>It depends on the operating system.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Case sensitivity is a MATLAB language rule, independent of the OS.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="no">
					<statement><p>They are the same if they hold the same value.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Names are compared character by character; values are irrelevant to naming.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</task>

		<!-- Q7 T/F -->
		<task label="vars-rq-7">
			<statement correct="yes">
				<p>
					A variable created with a semicolon at the end of the line still appears in
					the MATLAB workspace.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					True. The semicolon only suppresses Command Window output; the variable is
					fully created and stored in the workspace.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</task>

		<!-- Q8 T/F -->
		<task label="vars-rq-8">
			<statement correct="no">
				<p>
					In MATLAB, writing <pf>area = base * height</pf> creates a dynamic link so
					that <pf>area</pf> updates automatically whenever <pf>base</pf> or
					<pf>height</pf> changes.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					False. MATLAB evaluates the right-hand side once at the time of the assignment
					and stores the resulting number. No dynamic link is maintained.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</task>

		<!-- Q9 T/F -->
		<task label="vars-rq-9">
			<statement correct="no">
				<p>
					A variable name in MATLAB can begin with an underscore.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					False. MATLAB variable names must begin with a letter. An underscore is allowed
					within the name but not as the first character.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</task>

		<!-- Q10 Fill-in -->
		<task label="vars-rq-10">
			<statement>
				<p>
					After running the three commands below, what value is stored in <pf>p</pf>?
					Enter the number only.
				</p>
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
p = 5;
q = p + 3;
p = 2;
</code>
				</program>
				<p><fillin mode="number" answer="2"/></p>
			</statement>
			<evaluation>
				<evaluate>
					<test><numcmp use-answer="yes"/></test>
					<test><strcmp>.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect. The last assignment <pf>p = 2</pf> overwrites the earlier value.</p></feedback></test>
				</evaluate>
			</evaluation>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<!-- ============================================================ -->
	<!--	5. Coding Exercises											-->
	<!-- ============================================================ -->
	<exercise xml:id="variables-coding-exercises">
		<title>Coding Exercises</title>

		<task xml:id="vars-ex-1">
			<title>Speed, Distance, and Time</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write a MATLAB script that stores a car's speed and travel time in variables,
					then computes the distance traveled using <m>d = s \cdot t</m>.
				</p>

				<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
					<col width="11%" halign="right" />
					<col width="15%" halign="left" />
					<col width="28%" halign="center" />
					<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
					<row>
						<cell>Inputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>speed</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>speed in miles per hour</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell></cell>
						<cell><p><pf>time</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>travel time in hours</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell>Outputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>distance</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>distance traveled in miles</p></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>

				<p>
					Test with <pf>speed = 60</pf> and <pf>time = 2.5</pf>; the result should be
					<pf>distance = 150</pf>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
speed	= 60;
time	 = 2.5;
distance = speed * time
</code>
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>

		<task xml:id="vars-ex-2">
			<title>Celsius to Fahrenheit</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Convert a temperature from Celsius to Fahrenheit using the formula
					<m>F = \tfrac{9}{5}C + 32</m>.
				</p>

				<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
					<col width="11%" halign="right" />
					<col width="15%" halign="left" />
					<col width="28%" halign="center" />
					<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
					<row>
						<cell>Inputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>tempC</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>temperature in degrees Celsius</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell>Outputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>tempF</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>temperature in degrees Fahrenheit</p></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>

				<p>
					Test with <pf>tempC = 100</pf>; the result should be <pf>tempF = 212</pf>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
tempC = 100;
tempF = (9/5) * tempC + 32
</code>
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>

		<task xml:id="vars-ex-3">
			<title>Compound Interest — One Period</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute the balance after one year of compound interest using
					<m>A = P(1 + r)^n</m>, where <m>P</m> is the principal, <m>r</m> is the
					annual interest rate, and <m>n</m> is the number of years.
				</p>

				<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
					<col width="11%" halign="right" />
					<col width="15%" halign="left" />
					<col width="28%" halign="center" />
					<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
					<row>
						<cell>Inputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>principal</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>initial deposit in dollars</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell><p><pf>rate</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>annual interest rate (e.g., 0.05 for 5%)</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell></cell>
						<cell><p><pf>years</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>number of years</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell>Outputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>balance</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>account balance after the given years</p></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>

				<p>
					Test with <pf>principal = 1000</pf>, <pf>rate = 0.05</pf>,
					<pf>years = 3</pf>; the result should be approximately
					<pf>balance = 1157.6</pf>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
principal = 1000;
rate		= 0.05;
years	 = 3;
balance	 = principal * (1 + rate)^years
</code>
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>

		<task xml:id="vars-ex-4">
			<title>Area and Perimeter of a Rectangle</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Given the length and width of a rectangle, compute both its area and
					perimeter using descriptively named variables.
				</p>

				<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
					<col width="11%" halign="right" />
					<col width="15%" halign="left" />
					<col width="28%" halign="center" />
					<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
					<row>
						<cell>Inputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>rectLen</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>length of the rectangle</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell></cell>
						<cell><p><pf>rectWid</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>width of the rectangle</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>Outputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>rectArea</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>area of the rectangle</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell></cell>
						<cell><p><pf>rectPerim</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>perimeter of the rectangle</p></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>

				<p>
					Test with <pf>rectLen = 5</pf> and <pf>rectWid = 3</pf>; results should be
					<pf>rectArea = 15</pf> and <pf>rectPerim = 16</pf>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
rectLen	 = 5;
rectWid	 = 3;
rectArea	= rectLen * rectWid
rectPerim = 2 * (rectLen + rectWid)
</code>
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>

		<task xml:id="vars-ex-5">
			<title>Kinetic Energy</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute the kinetic energy of a moving object using
					<m>KE = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2</m>.
				</p>

				<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
					<col width="11%" halign="right" />
					<col width="15%" halign="left" />
					<col width="28%" halign="center" />
					<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
					<row>
						<cell>Inputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>mass</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>mass of the object in kilograms</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell></cell>
						<cell><p><pf>velocity</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>speed of the object in meters per second</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell>Outputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>kineticEnergy</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>kinetic energy in joules</p></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>

				<p>
					Test with <pf>mass = 2</pf> and <pf>velocity = 3</pf>; the result should be
					<pf>kineticEnergy = 9</pf>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<program language="matlab">
<code>
mass			= 2;
velocity		= 3;
kineticEnergy = 0.5 * mass * velocity^2
</code>
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>

	</exercise>

</section>